    <statement><p>Find <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that <m>125, x, y, 1</m> is part of an arithmetic sequence.</p>
<p><m>x =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0001"/>, <m>y =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p>Then find <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> so that the sequence is part of a geometric sequence.</p>
<p><m>x =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0003"/>, <m>y =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
<p>(Warning: <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> might not be integers.)</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>83.6667</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>83.6667-41.3333</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>125^{\frac{2}{3}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>125^{\frac{1}{3}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>For arithmetic: <m>x = 83.6666666666667\text{,}</m> <m>y = 42.3333333333333\text{.}</m> For the arithmetic sequence, we know <m>125 + d = x\text{,}</m> <m>x + d = y\text{,}</m> and <m>y + d = 1\text{.}</m>  In other words, <m>125 + 3d = 1</m> so <m>d = (-41.3333333333333)/3\text{.}</m>
For geometric: <m>x = {25}</m> and <m>y = {5}\text{.}</m>    Similarly we can find the common ratio for the geometric sequence by solving <m>125\cdot r^3 = 1</m> for <m>r\text{.}</m></p>
</solution>
